README file for Programming Assignment I (Lexical Analysis - cool.flex)
========================================================================

1. Overview
-----------
This assignment implements a lexical analyzer (scanner) for the COOL (Classroom
Object-Oriented Language) using GNU Flex. The scanner reads COOL source files,
tokenizes the input according to the Cool Reference Manual lexical specification,
builds entries in the string, integer, and identifier tables, and communicates
tokens and errors to the parser via `cool_yylex()`.

2. Design & Implementation Details
----------------------------------

a. Keywords & Identifiers:
   - All Cool keywords (except `true` and `false`) are case-insensitive. They
     are matched using full case-insensitive regex patterns (e.g. `[cC][lL][aA][sS][sS]`).
   - `true` and `false` must begin with a lowercase letter ('t' or 'f'), with
     remaining characters being case-insensitive (e.g. `t[rR][uU][eE]`). They set
     `cool_yylval.boolean` to 1 or 0 respectively and return `BOOL_CONST`.
   - Identifiers beginning with a capital letter `[A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*` return
     `TYPEID` and are stored in `idtable`.
   - Identifiers beginning with a lowercase letter `[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*` return
     `OBJECTID` and are stored in `idtable`.
   - Integer constants `[0-9]+` return `INT_CONST` and are stored in `inttable`.

b. Comments:
   - Single-line comments starting with `--` are ignored up to the end of the line
     without consuming the newline character, ensuring accurate `curr_lineno`
     tracking.
   - Multi-line comments enclosed in `(* ... *)` support arbitrary levels of nesting.
     A global counter `comment_depth` tracks nesting depth within the `%x COMMENT`
     start condition:
       - `(*` increments `comment_depth`.
       - `*)` decrements `comment_depth` and exits back to `INITIAL` when depth hits 0.
       - Reaching EOF inside a comment emits the error "EOF in comment".
       - Unmatched `*)` outside a comment in `INITIAL` emits the error "Unmatched *)".

c. String Constants:
   - Handled via exclusive start conditions `%x STRING` and `%x STRING_ERROR`.
   - String constants accumulate into `string_buf` with maximum length 1024 characters
     (leaving 1 character for null terminator `\0` within `MAX_STR_CONST = 1025`).
   - If the string exceeds 1024 characters, the error "String constant too long" is
     reported and the scanner enters `STRING_ERROR`.
   - If an unescaped newline is found within a string, `curr_lineno` is incremented,
     the error "Unterminated string constant" is reported, and the scanner returns to
     `INITIAL`.
   - If a literal null character (`\0`) is encountered, the error "String contains
     null character" is reported and the scanner enters `STRING_ERROR`.
   - In accordance with the Cool specification, the escape sequence `\0` (the two
     characters '\' and '0') is explicitly converted to the character '0' (ASCII 48).
   - Standard escape sequences `\b`, `\t`, `\n`, `\f`, and `\c` (for any character c)
     are converted to their respective characters. Escaped newlines `\\n` increment
     `curr_lineno` and append `\n`.
   - In `STRING_ERROR`, remaining characters are discarded until either a closing quote
     or an unescaped newline is reached, ensuring subsequent tokens are scanned cleanly.
   - EOF within a string produces "EOF in string constant".

d. Error Recovery & Invalid Characters:
   - Any character not matching valid Cool lexical syntax triggers the catch-all
     rule `.`, returning `ERROR` with `cool_yylval.error_msg = yytext` and resuming
     at the next character.

3. Testing Strategy (`test.cl`)
-------------------------------
`test.cl` thoroughly tests:
1. Single-line and deeply nested multi-line comments.
2. Case-insensitive keywords and proper precedence between keywords and identifiers.
3. Boolean constants vs capitalized Type Identifiers (`True`, `False`).
4. All compound operators (`=>`, `<-`, `<=`) and single-character tokens.
5. String constants, all escape sequences, multi-line escaped strings, and length checks.
6. Real-world COOL classes containing complex expressions, loops, branches, and blocks.

